    ESP8266 Communication with Raspberry Pi:
        Use ESP8266 to gather sensor data and communicate directly with the Raspberry Pi in each building.
        ESP8266 can send data to the Raspberry Pi via Wi-Fi using TCP/IP or UDP communication protocols. This can be achieved using HTTP requests or MQTT protocol.
        MQTT (Message Queuing Telemetry Transport) is a lightweight messaging protocol that's ideal for IoT applications. It's efficient and supports both publishing and subscribing to topics, making it suitable for sensor data transmission.
        You can use popular ESP8266 libraries like PubSubClient to implement MQTT communication.

    Raspberry Pi Setup:
        Set up each Raspberry Pi to run a Python script or a Node.js application that listens for incoming data from ESP8266.
        Parse the data received from ESP8266 and organize it into a suitable format.
        Once the data is collected and organized, make HTTP POST requests or send data through MQTT to the main server.

    Main Server Setup:
        Set up the main server with an Express.js web server to handle incoming data from all buildings.
        Implement an endpoint to receive data from Raspberry Pis in each building.
        Upon receiving data, store it in a database or process it as required by your application.
        You can also implement authentication and authorization mechanisms if needed to ensure secure communication between buildings and the main server.

    Web Application:
        Develop a web application that fetches data from the main server and presents it in a user-friendly interface.
        Use technologies like React.js, Angular, or Vue.js for frontend development.
        The web application can provide visualization, analytics, and control functionalities based on the sensor data received.
